WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:02.958
(bright music)

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.090
- What is in-context learning?

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.370
Slightly related to the previous question

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.810
and thinking about the kinds of things

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.430
that language models can do,

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.620
or especially that we
were surprised to see

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.060
that language models can do.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.310
Maybe one of the most
surprising was this phenomenon

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.450
called in-context learning

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.682
where if I take a little
machine learning data set

00:00:23.682 --> 00:00:26.190
and I just feed it to the
language model, right?

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.890
So I give it a movie review
and then the star rating

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.780
that was assigned to
the movie by the critic,

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.060
and then another movie
review and the star rating

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.760
that was assigned to
that movie by the critic,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.580
and you give just a couple
of examples of these things.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.410
Language models generate the ability

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.680
both to generate plausible
sounding movie reviews,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.120
but also to predict the star ratings

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.530
that should come after them, right?

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.570
And more generally, if I have
a machine learning problem.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.520
I have my inputs and my outputs,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.760
I put some inputs and outputs
as input to the model,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.550
I give it one more input and I
ask it to predict the output,

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.350
models can often do this really well.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.860
And this is a super interesting,
fundamentally different way

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.750
of doing machine learning, right,

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.940
where I have this one
big general purpose model

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:06.960
into which I can insert

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.510
lots of little machine
learning data sets and yet,

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.460
without having to train
a new model at all,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.710
classifier or a generator or whatever

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.480
specialized to my particular task.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:16.980
This is actually something
we've been thinking a lot

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.380
about in my group and
in some collaborations

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.630
with colleagues at Google
trying to understand exactly

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.940
how this in-context learning phenomenon

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:24.990
actually comes about.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.350
What are your thoughts
on the potential of LLMs,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.770
should people be scared,
excited, or somewhere in between?

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.780
I think the answer is
somewhere in between.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.400
Certainly, in the short-term,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.650
the thing that I'm most scared about

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.930
has to do with these sort of truthfulness

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.660
and coherence issues that I
was mentioning before, right?

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.790
That even the best
models that we have today

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.360
do generate incorrect facts,
they generate code with bugs,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.580
and sort of because of
the way these models work,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.010
they do so in a way that's
particularly difficult

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.220
for humans to spot
because the model output

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.780
has all the right surface statistics.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.520
And so when we think about code,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.100
I think it's still an open question,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.510
whether it's actually less work

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.490
for somebody to write a function by hand

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.100
or to ask language model
to generate that function

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.240
and then have the person go through

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.460
and verify that the
implementation of that function

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.510
was actually correct.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.910
And I think there's a
little danger in rushing

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.712
to deploy these tools right away

00:02:16.712 --> 00:02:18.510
that will wind up in a world

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.430
where everything's a little bit worse

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.230
where it's actually very
difficult for people

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.790
to actually reliably check
the outputs of these models.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:27.623
That being said,

00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:29.550
I think these are problems
that can be overcome.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.730
I think the pace that things
are moving at especially,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.530
there's a lot of room
to address these issues

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.930
of factuality and
coherence and correctness

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.820
of generated code and things like that.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.920
And that in the long term,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.850
these really are tools I
think for freeing ourselves up

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:48.960
as a society from a lot of sort
of unpleasant or drudge work

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.520
that has been difficult to automate,

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.820
and that that's something
to be excited about.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.360
How to deal with ethics issues

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.580
if generative AI generate data

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.310
and the fake data is misused in some way?

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.500
We deal with these ethics
issues in the same way

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.500
that we've always done
with legal processes

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.910
and societal processes
and governmental processes

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.070
that are designed to address them.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.170
At the end of the day,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.550
people are using these systems, right?

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.230
They're not fully autonomous,

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.870
there are people who are
responsible for their inputs,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.010
responsible for their outputs,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.690
responsible for their behaviors.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.170
And I think thinking that,

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.660
the sort of issues associated
with the ethical deployment

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.690
of these technologies is
something that we as technologists

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.180
can solve is really misguided.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.057
And that in fact, there are
already sophisticated structures

00:03:40.057 --> 00:03:43.110
in place in our societies and cultures

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.840
for dealing with factual issues,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.240
for dealing with generation of untruths

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.731
or unreliable or incorrect
outputs or bad machine decisions.

00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:54.780
And that we really need to think

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.210
about strengthening these systems

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.640
rather than trying to come up
with technological solutions

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.340
or purely technological solutions.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.310
Why do LLMs tend to hallucinate facts

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.250
that is confidently assert
things that are not true,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.140
and does that limit the
usefulness of these models

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.380
for applications where
factual accuracy is important?

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.743
We've alluded to this hallucination
issue a couple of times,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:23.030
and I think why it happens is
still partly an open question.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.084
But if I had to guess,
right, just architecturally,

00:04:26.084 --> 00:04:29.580
there are reasons that
these big transformer models

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.690
that it's hard for these models

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.300
to build coherent models
of the world, right?

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.590
And they can do that a little bit.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.680
You can sort of query them
with factual questions,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.020
trivia questions, and they get
'em right most of the time,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.100
maybe even more often

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.830
than your average human
user off the street.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:45.300
But unlike your average human user,

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.910
it's really unclear
whether there's anything

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.410
that lives inside this language model

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.500
that corresponds to a belief
about the state of the world.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.200
And I think this is both
for architectural reasons,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.600
that just transformers don't
obviously have anywhere

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.580
to put that belief, and training data,

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.520
that these models are
trained on the internet

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.791
which was authored by a
bunch of different people

00:05:04.791 --> 00:05:06.990
at different moments who
believe different things

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.040
about the state of the world,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.419
and therefore it's
difficult to expect models

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:12.750
to represent those things coherently.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.010
All that being said,

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.171
I don't think this is a
fundamental limitation

00:05:17.171 --> 00:05:18.930
of neural language models

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.970
or even more general
language models in general

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.139
but something that's true
about today's language models,

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:25.680
we're already seeing that models

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:27.900
are sort of approaching being able

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.140
to build representations of fact,

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.000
representations of the state of the world,

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.370
and I think there's
room to improve further.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.666
Is it ethical for these
systems to use the first person

00:05:37.666 --> 00:05:39.803
when interacting with users?

00:05:39.803 --> 00:05:43.170
Should they respond with,
"As an AI language model, I,"

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.810
or something like this generative
text prediction system?

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:46.800
This is a great question.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.980
So I certainly believe that
it's very, very, very important

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.210
for deployed versions of
these systems to make it clear

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:55.860
to users that they're interacting

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.640
with some automated
decision making process

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.040
and not an actual human
user on the other end,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.300
and that kind of transparency

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.580
is really fundamental for getting people

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.100
to trust these systems and
understand their limitations.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.530
I don't think it's necessarily a problem

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.155
for these models to use the word I,

00:06:13.155 --> 00:06:14.850
that they don't have
to refer to themselves

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:17.580
as this generative text
prediction system every time.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.080
I think we're already used to dealing

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.731
with personal assistance
like Siri or Alexa or Cortana

00:06:22.731 --> 00:06:25.860
that refer to themselves
in the first person.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.180
But I think there are ways of doing that

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.300
while still making it
extremely clear to the user

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.100
and maybe reminding them periodically

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.650
that what they're interacting
with is not a person.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.050
Is it possible for LLMs

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.120
to contain mini neural networks within

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.030
and adaptively use these
mini neural networks

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:43.140
for in-context learning?

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:44.520
This is a great question

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.970
and related to the
in-context learning question

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.020
that we had before.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.930
And so I'll give specifically
a shout out to my student,

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.320
in Accurex work in collaboration
with folks at Google,

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.240
Amy answered exactly this question

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.520
and showing that at least in principle,

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.890
it is possible for
transformer language models

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.890
to train mini machine
learning models inside of them

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.210
and simulate that training process

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.120
and use the embedded
models to make predictions.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.830
Whether this is actually what's going on

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.751
in the larger scale models
that we have right now

00:07:13.751 --> 00:07:17.940
is an empirical question,
whether that little mini model

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.100
that's getting simulated
during in-context learning,

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.960
is it transformer, or something smaller,

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.730
or something simpler, something different?

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:27.480
All still unknown, but this
is an active area of research,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.680
and one that I'm
particularly excited about.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.620
Is it possible that an LLM is fine tuned

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:35.820
with other kinds of data,

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.030
for example, time series
data and perform well?

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.370
Yeah, so this is also a great question.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.280
And I think right now when people think

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:47.746
about applying language
models to other kinds,

00:07:47.746 --> 00:07:52.470
data in other modalities,
other problem domains,

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.750
they are thinking about
converting those problem domains

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.420
into text querying the
language model in tech space

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:58.980
and then getting the answers back out.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.350
But this is not the only
thing that you can do

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.230
and it's definitely
possible to take a model

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:05.970
that's been pre-trained on language data

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.250
and fine tune it on data
and other modalities.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.620
And what's especially
interesting there is that,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.450
if you succeed at doing this right,

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.950
then that tells you
that the language model

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:16.020
is learned something,
not just about language,

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.360
but about kind of structured
data sets in general.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.080
So here at MIT, one version
of this that we've done,

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.330
my group in collaboration
with Antonio Torralba's group

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.060
and actually also some folks at Nvidia,

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:28.740
is look specifically at what happens

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.660
when you take language
models and fine tune them

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.160
on robot demonstrations.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.235
And there you do actually
really seem to get improvements

00:08:35.235 --> 00:08:37.320
in model's ability to sort of generalize

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.180
to new tasks and new goals.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.440
And I think that's some evidence

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.930
that pre-training on language data

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:44.490
tells you something not
just about language,

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.683
but about structure in general.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.869
LLMs are getting a lot
of attention right now,

00:08:50.869 --> 00:08:54.420
but what other new AI or ML models

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.250
are also seeing meaningful progress

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:59.885
and should we keep in
mind in the coming years?

00:08:59.885 --> 00:09:04.020
In terms of sort of basic
neural network architectures

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.240
that we have for solving
prediction problems,

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.370
these sort of like one token at a time,

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.170
what are called auto regressive models

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.080
are the rage in NLP right now.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.870
But people who work with
other kinds of data modalities

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.060
still use different kinds of models.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:20.220
Obviously, in image generation land,

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.410
there's a huge amount of
interest in diffusion models

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.660
that underlie a lot of the
image generation results

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.190
that people are seeing right now.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.530
There's still a lot of use of
convolutional neural networks,

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.450
and recently, there's been
quite a bit of interest

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:35.040
in looking at ways of applying tools

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.680
that people use right now mostly
for working with image data

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.750
and using them to work
with text data instead.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.250
And I guess looking a bit past

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:45.990
the low level neural modeling questions

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:48.474
that a lot of people are
thinking about right now,

00:09:48.474 --> 00:09:50.220
it wouldn't actually surprise me

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:54.450
if we see a return to
some of the more explicit

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.600
structured approaches that
were at least more predominant

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:00.900
in a previous generation
of NLP approaches, right?

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.730
So there's this whole long tradition

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.850
of formal representations
of meanings of sentences

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:07.980
basically as chunks of code

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:11.850
that you can sort of answer a user's query

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.190
by not just pulling the
answer to that query

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:15.270
out of a language model

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:18.060
but translating it into a database query

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.820
or something even more abstract,
a little computer program

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.280
that you can run and
execute to get answers out.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.900
And one of the things
that's really appealing

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.220
about language models

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.333
or language to code models in particular

00:10:28.333 --> 00:10:31.050
is that they actually make
it a lot easier to implement

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.190
a lot of these kind of classical

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:33.150
structured approaches, right?

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.240
So if you sort of take very
seriously linguistic theories

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.370
about how sentences
ought to get represented,

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.380
now you can implement those
theories computationally

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:42.780
not by sort of spending an entire PhD

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.430
handcrafting some kind of model,

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:46.530
but just by showing a language model.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.690
A couple of examples of
the kind of representation

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:49.830
that you're interested in.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:51.750
So thinking back to what we said before

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.760
about language models
maybe not being great

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.290
at building models of the world

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:58.080
or solving really complicated computation

00:10:58.080 --> 00:10:59.730
or inference problems, there,

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:01.260
I think there's actually a lot of room

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.210
to use language models
just as a building block

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:06.330
in more complicated systems
or more sophisticated systems

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:08.340
that map from language to to code

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.702
or other kinds of structured
representations of meaning

00:11:10.702 --> 00:11:14.103
as a stepping stone towards final answers.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.130
If GPT-5 was a student of yours,

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.113
how well would it do in your classes?

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.740
So, well, GPT-5 isn't out yet,

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:24.150
so I don't think any of us knows

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.740
quite what it's gonna be able to do.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.110
